<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:data="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Data.Converters;assembly=Avalonia.Base"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="1000" d:DesignHeight="620"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.KeyboardEditorView"
             x:DataType="vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel">
  <!-- The per-device editor of specs/10-apps-and-ui.md, on the redesign's editor-shell layout
       (docs/design/handoff.md § "Editor shell", mockups 1e/1f/2a): a 46px toolbar, a 38px tab bar,
       the Demo Mode bar, then the content. The keyboard picture the remap workflow runs on, the
       macro library of the Macros tab, the Lighting and Settings panels, and the feature panels of
       specs/11-feature-dialogs.md rendered as an overlay over all of it. The picture itself is the
       generic KeyboardView; everything device-specific reaches it as data.

       LAYOUT SHAPE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF ISSUE #128's FIRST HALF. The chrome is FULL-BLEED — the
       toolbar, the tab bar and the Demo Mode bar run edge to edge — and below it the screen is
       split into COLUMNS rather than rows: the right-hand rail is one column that runs from the top
       of the advisory row to the bottom of the window, and everything else — the advisory strip,
       the board, the action row, the invalid-line block — lives in the column beside it.

         Row 0  toolbar        46  Home | device | mount path .......... Save | status chip
         Row 1  tab bar        38  Layout Macros [Lighting] [Settings] ... profile · LAYER (segments)
         Row 2  Demo Mode bar      only in demo mode
         Row 3  the body           three columns:
                  Col 0            the advisory strip (Auto) over the padded content (*)
                  Col 1            the drag seam
                  Col 2            the rail — key inspector on Keys, mode rail on Lighting

       WHY THE BODY IS A GRID OF ITS OWN rather than four more rows of the root with the rail
       spanning two of them. A Grid distributes a SPANNING child's desired size across the
       definitions it spans, and only Auto ones can grow — so a rail spanning the advisory row and
       the content row could push the 30px advisory row taller at a small window, and a full-bleed
       toolbar spanning the star board column plus the Auto seam column could widen the seam. The
       nested body has no spans at all, so neither distribution can happen, and the arrangement on
       screen is exactly the one described above.

       WHAT MOVED, AND WHY (issue #128):

         · The advisory strip is the BOARD COLUMN's width, not the window's. It annotates the
           section, and the section is what is left of the rail; a bar that ran on under the rail
           read as chrome for the whole editor. Still ONE strip and one view model instance for all
           four tabs (deviation 18) — its width changed, not its cardinality.
         · The rail is OUTSIDE the padded content area, so the 16px the content is inset by no
           longer shows as dead space above and below it.
         · The provisional action row and the invalid-line block sit UNDER THE BOARD, inside the
           board column, instead of under the board *and* the rail.
         · On Macros and Settings the rail column and the seam collapse to zero width — both those
           sections take the whole content width and always did.

       The root Panel with the overlay scrim as a sibling of the grid is load-bearing: the spec 11
       panels render into it. -->
  <Panel>
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*">

      <!-- ===== Row 0: the editor toolbar ====================================================
           It REPLACES the shell's app bar rather than sitting under it: the mockups draw exactly
           one 46px bar while editing, carrying the same things. MainWindow hides its own for as
           long as an editor ProvidesOwnChrome (MainWindowViewModel.IsShellChromeVisible).

           NO EJECT BUTTON, though mockup 1e draws one. "Nothing ejects implicitly" — eject is the
           dashboard card's own deliberate action (issue #88). Recorded as a deliberate deviation
           in docs/app/design-system.md.

           NO PROFILE CAPTION EITHER, since issue #128. It used to be a muted `Profile 1` beside the
           mount path — a read-only label for the one thing on this screen the user most wants to
           change. The profile is a DROP-DOWN in the tab-bar row now, and a label repeating the
           dropdown's own selection two rows above it would be the third place the app said the same
           word.

           Home and the status chip come from the shell through `Shell` (IShellChrome), never from
           a $parent[MainWindow] lookup: every headless suite renders this view standalone, where
           such a lookup resolves to nothing and both would render blank. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="0"
              Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceBarBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}"
              BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
              Height="{DynamicResource HeightToolbar}"
              Padding="16,0">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="12" VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <Button Content="Home" Classes="secondary" Command="{Binding Shell.HomeCommand}" />

            <Border Width="1"
                    Height="20"
                    Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding DeviceName}" Classes="toolbarDevice" VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- The mount path is a value that exists verbatim on the machine, so it is mono (the
                 mono law). Hidden in demo mode and on a device with no drive, where there is no
                 path to name. -->
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding MountPath}"
                       Classes="monoValue muted"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasMountPath}" />
          </StackPanel>

          <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="12" VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding RemapCounterCaption}" Classes="meta muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding MacroCounterCaption}" Classes="meta muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel.SavingCaption}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" />

            <!-- Save "turns amber whenever the session is dirty" (handoff § Editor shell). The
                 class is all this view says; the amber ramp is painted inside the
                 PrimaryActionButton control theme, which is the only layer allowed to name a
                 template part. -->
            <Button Content="Save"
                    Classes="primaryAction"
                    Classes.dirty="{Binding IsDirty}"
                    Command="{Binding SaveCommand}" />

            <views:StatusChipView DataContext="{Binding Shell}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== Row 1: the tab bar =============================================================
           The TabStrip control theme already carries the fixed 38 height, the inset face, the
           hairline under it, the padding and the 500-weight-plus-2px-accent-underline active state,
           so it is used as it is and paints the whole bar: it is stretched across the row and the
           row's right-hand controls are laid OVER its right end, rather than the bar being
           re-implemented as a Border with a transparent strip inside it.

           The strip carries only sections that work — a feature the board lacks is not rendered at
           all (EditorTabViewModel.CreateAll), which is why there is no longer a scoped TabStripItem
           style binding IsEnabled here. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="1">
        <!-- A real TabStrip: the header row of a tab control with no content host, which is exactly
             what this is — the strip selects, and the Panel below toggles which section is visible.
             `SelectedValue` rides the view model's own EditorTab, mapped through each entry's Tab,
             so the strip and the section stay one value; the click still runs SelectTabCommand. -->
        <TabStrip Theme="{StaticResource TabStrip}"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding Tabs}"
                  SelectedValueBinding="{Binding Tab, DataType=vm:EditorTabViewModel}"
                  SelectedValue="{Binding SelectedTab, Mode=OneWay}"
                  SelectionChanged="OnTabSelectionChanged">
          <TabStrip.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate DataType="vm:EditorTabViewModel">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" />
            </DataTemplate>
          </TabStrip.ItemTemplate>
        </TabStrip>

        <!-- The bar's right end: the profile the editor has open, then whichever layer switch the
             open section owns. Horizontal, so nothing here can ever become a vertical StackPanel
             above a board. -->
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Spacing="12"
                    Margin="0,0,12,0"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">

          <!-- ===== The profile picker (issue #128) ==========================================
               A numbered profile is what the whole editor is looking at, and until now the only
               way to change it was to close the board and come back. It is a drop-down rather than
               another segmented switch because a device carries up to nine of them, and it is on
               EVERY tab because a profile is not a layer: switching it reloads the layout, the led
               file and the settings alike, so no section is unaffected by it.

               `SelectedItem` reads the view model ONE WAY — SelectProfileCommand is the only thing
               allowed to move it, exactly as with the two switches beside it — and the selection
               change runs that command through the code-behind. -->
          <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding Profiles}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedProfile, Mode=OneWay}"
                    SelectionChanged="OnProfileSelectionChanged"
                    IsVisible="{Binding HasProfiles}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:ProfileOptionViewModel">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>

          <!-- The LAYOUT layer switch, built from the model's layers, so it is two segments on a
               Freestyle Edge RGB and five on an Advantage 360 (issue #41).

               A ListBox rather than a row of buttons: the switch is one-of-N, which is what a
               SelectingItemsControl is, and it buys the whole group one tab stop with arrow-key
               movement inside it. `SelectedItem` reads the view model one way — the property is
               read-only, and SelectLayer is the only thing allowed to move it — while the selection
               change runs the very command the buttons used to.

               NOT ON THE LIGHTING TAB. There are two different "layers" in this app and they are
               not the same object: this one is the LAYOUT layer (layoutN.txt, what a key does), and
               the Lighting tab has the LED file's layer (ledN.txt, what a key looks like). Drawn
               together they are two identical Top/Fn segment pairs a few hundred pixels apart, and
               only one of them does anything while lighting is open — so this one yields the slot,
               and the lighting switch below takes it. -->
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                      Spacing="8"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter='Keys,Macros,Settings'}">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:KeyboardEditorView.LayerSwitchLabel}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <ListBox Theme="{StaticResource SegmentedControl}"
                     ItemsSource="{Binding Layers}"
                     SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedLayer, Mode=OneWay}"
                     SelectionChanged="OnLayerSelectionChanged">
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:KeyboardLayerViewModel">
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <!-- The ⌥n legend of mockup 1f. The legend is display text; the accelerator it
                         names is wired by the editor's keyboard grammar, which maps ⌥ to Alt on
                         every platform (KinesisEdit.Input.EditorShortcuts).

                         The ⌥ is DRAWN, not typed: U+2325 is in neither embedded IBM Plex family,
                         so the character would be tofu on the one platform that shows it. The
                         chip's Content is therefore a panel of mark-plus-text rather than a string
                         — its template ends in a plain ContentPresenter, so this needs nothing from
                         the ControlTheme and names no template part. Windows and Linux hide the
                         mark and read `Alt+1`; the mark cannot inherit the chip's Foreground (an
                         Icon paints from Stroke), which is what `Icon.kbdMark` in
                         Styles/Editor.axaml is for. -->
                    <ContentControl Classes="kbd" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2">
                        <controls:Icon Classes="kbdMark"
                                       Data="{DynamicResource IconOption}"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding ShowsOptionMark}"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShortcutHint}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      </StackPanel>
                    </ContentControl>
                  </StackPanel>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- ===== The LIGHTING layer switch (issue #128) ====================================
               2f draws one switch on this screen, relabelled "Lighting layer", and it used to be
               drawn by LightingTabView itself — above the board, inside the board column. That put
               the editor's one switcher slot in two different places depending on the tab, and cost
               the board a row of height it did not have at the window floor. It is the same slot as
               the layout switch now, and it is still a DIFFERENT OBJECT: ledN.txt's layer, not
               layoutN.txt's, which is why the label keeps saying so.

               The Fn segment below the LED 1.0.44 gate stays visible and dimmed (specs/07 §3)
               rather than being hidden — the layer exists — and the reason moved onto the switch as
               its tooltip, because there is no room beside it in a 38px bar for the sentence
               LightingTabView used to print. `ToolTip.ServiceEnabled` is what keeps an available
               switch from opening an empty tip: LayerLockHint is the empty string when the gate is
               open, and an empty string is not a null Tip. -->
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                      Spacing="8"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Lighting}">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:KeyboardEditorView.LightingLayerSwitchLabel}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <ListBox Theme="{StaticResource SegmentedControl}"
                     ItemsSource="{Binding Lighting.Layers}"
                     SelectedItem="{Binding Lighting.SelectedLayer, Mode=OneWay}"
                     SelectionChanged="OnLightingLayerSelectionChanged"
                     ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Lighting.LayerLockHint}"
                     ToolTip.ServiceEnabled="{Binding !Lighting.IsLayerCustomizationAvailable}"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <!-- Scoped to this list, so it cannot outrank the layout switch's container theme. -->
              <ListBox.Styles>
                <Style Selector="ListBoxItem" x:DataType="vm:LightingLayerViewModel">
                  <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding IsEnabled}" />
                </Style>
              </ListBox.Styles>
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingLayerViewModel">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" />
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>

      <!-- ===== Row 2: the Demo Mode bar =======================================================
           Demo mode never touched the drive and can never save (specs/03-vdrive-and-files.md §3.5).
           Purple, not amber: "nothing is written" is its own state in the design's four-status
           vocabulary, and amber is reserved for advisories.

           BOTH of mockup 1f's actions, in its order: `Export layout to file…` then
           `Connect a device`. Export is live here — a demo board with fixtures opens a real session
           over the synthetic v-Drive, and an export writes to a folder the user picked rather than
           to the drive (specs/11-feature-dialogs.md §11.5), so 03 §3.5 is untouched by it. Save and
           Import are the two that stay refused, and neither is drawn on this bar. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="2"
              Background="{DynamicResource StatusDemoTintBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusDemoTintBorderBrush}"
              BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
              Padding="24,8"
              IsVisible="{Binding IsDemoMode}">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{x:Static vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel.DemoModeBarMessage}"
                     Classes="body statusDemo"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />

          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <Button Content="{x:Static vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel.DemoModeExportCaption}"
                    Classes="secondary"
                    Command="{Binding ExportCommand}" />

            <Button Content="{x:Static vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel.DemoModeConnectCaption}"
                    Classes="secondary"
                    Command="{Binding Shell.HomeCommand}" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== Row 3: the body ================================================================
           THREE COLUMNS: the section, the drag seam, and the rail beside them.

           THE RAIL'S COLUMN IS WHERE ITS WIDTH LIVES (issue #119). It used to be a style setter on
           `Border.inspectorRail`, and the rail used to collapse itself when nothing was selected so
           this column measured zero — which is what made the board jump sideways to re-centre. The
           rail is permanent now, and the width had to MOVE onto the column rather than be layered
           over the setter: a GridSplitter resizes columns, and a local Width on the rail would have
           outranked whatever the column said.

           The column is driven from code-behind (Views/InspectorRailColumn) rather than by a
           binding, because a ColumnDefinition is a bare AvaloniaObject: it is in neither the
           logical nor the visual tree, so it inherits no DataContext and a {Binding} on it has
           nothing to resolve against.

           IT IS ONE COLUMN FOR BOTH RAILS SINCE ISSUE #128. Issue #124 had already given the two
           rails one stored width, but they were still two columns in two grids — so the Lighting
           tab's rail started under its own tab's padding and the two were only ever the same size
           by arithmetic. There is one ColumnDefinition now and the rail's two faces are two
           IsVisible-gated children of it, which is why switching between Keys and Lighting cannot
           move the board by so much as a pixel. -->
      <Grid x:Name="EditorColumns" Grid.Row="3" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">

        <!-- ===== Column 0: the open section ===================================================
             The advisory strip over the padded content. The strip is NOT full-bleed any more
             (issue #128): it belongs to the section it annotates, and the section is what is left
             of the rail. Its own 24px padding is the content grid's 24px inset, so the dot and the
             sentence start on the same vertical line the board's own block does. -->
        <Grid Grid.Column="0" RowDefinitions="Auto,*">

          <!-- One strip serves all four tabs: its sentence and its count are the *open* section's,
               and the whole strip empties when that section has nothing to say — but the ROW IS
               RESERVED, NOT COLLAPSED (issue #119), so the board below it never shifts as the user
               edits. The row is Auto rather than 30 because the `advisory_detail` preference lets
               the strip wrap to its full text and grow; at its default (one trimmed line) the
               height is the same 30px it always was. -->
          <views:AdvisoryStripView Grid.Row="0" DataContext="{Binding AdvisoryStrip}" />

          <!-- The only padded region; everything above it is full-bleed chrome, and the rail beside
               it carries its own inset surface instead. -->
          <Grid Grid.Row="1" RowDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" Margin="24,16">

            <Panel Grid.Row="0">
              <TextBlock Text="Loading profile..."
                         Classes="body muted"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center"
                         IsVisible="{Binding IsLoading}" />

              <!-- A save is in flight: the picture stays on screen but nothing may be edited under
                   it. The rail carries the same gate on its own host, because it is no longer
                   inside this Panel. -->
              <Panel IsVisible="{Binding !IsLoading}" IsEnabled="{Binding !IsBusy}">
                <!-- The Layout tab: the keyboard canvas and its legend row. There is deliberately
                     NO clipping container anywhere between here and a cap: ClipToBounds is false
                     the whole way down the board chain (Controls/KeyboardView.axaml), and a Border
                     here would start cutting caps off.

                     THE BOARD IS THE LAYOUT TAB'S ALONE SINCE ISSUE #93. The Macros tab used to
                     dock its editor beside this picture; it is a library now (mockup 1i) and takes
                     the whole content area for itself. -->
                <Panel IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Keys}">
                  <!-- The listening banner, the keyboard canvas and its legend row, centred as ONE
                       block in the content area (handoff § "Screens → 3. Layout tab": "Content =
                       keyboard canvas (centered, both split halves as separate panels with 26px
                       gutter) + legend row"). The three share one container so the row is exactly as
                       wide as the board and its ends line up with the board's, which is how mockups
                       1e/2a draw it.

                       THE BANNER IS INSIDE THE BLOCK, and that is not tidiness: the block is what
                       the column centres, so a reserved band ABOVE it would push the picture half a
                       band below the middle of the screen for ever — which BoardFitTests reads as
                       an off-centre board and is right to. Inside, the band is part of the thing
                       being centred, the board keeps the middle of the space the screen has, and
                       the notice sits with the picture it is about.

                       IT IS A GRID, AND NOT A StackPanel, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ISSUE #123's
                       SECOND HALF. A vertically-oriented StackPanel measures its children with
                       double.PositiveInfinity down its own stack axis — so the BoardScaleHost
                       inside the picture saw no vertical constraint at all, took its scale from the
                       width alone, and reported a block taller than the slot it had been given.
                       Nothing clips on this chain (deliberately, for the focus halo), so the excess
                       was PAINTED OVER the action row beneath rather than cut: at 1600x520 the
                       legend row landed 18px past the bottom edge of the window. A star row hands
                       the picture the height that is actually there, minus the legend, which is
                       what "scale the whole board uniformly to fit the content area" (handoff §
                       "Avalonia implementation notes") asks for.

                       RULE: no vertically-oriented StackPanel may be an ancestor of a
                       BoardScaleHost. KeyboardViewTests walks the real views and fails if one is.

                       NO OUTER FRAME. The picture draws its own bordered section panels
                       (Controls/KeyboardView.axaml), so the `keyboardBoard` inset surround this
                       used to carry would be a second frame around them; the canvas here is the
                       content area itself. -->
                  <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        IsVisible="{Binding SelectedLayer, Converter={x:Static data:ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
                    <!--
                      THE LISTENING BANNER. No "Esc cancels" in it: while a key is listening the
                      capture service swallows every physical key, Escape included, so Escape is
                      assigned like any other key (a keyboard must be able to carry an Escape
                      remap). Cancelling is therefore a pointer action.

                      ITS ROW IS RESERVED, NOT COLLAPSED (issue #131), exactly as the advisory
                      strip's is (issue #119, Views/AdvisoryStripView.axaml, whose header carries
                      the full reasoning). This Panel carries no IsVisible: it holds the row whether
                      or not a key is listening, and only its CONTENTS come and go. Arming a key
                      used to grow the row from nothing to a 32px bar and its 8px of air, which
                      shrank the star row under it, made BoardScaleHost re-fit, and moved the board
                      20px down at the exact moment the user was looking at one cap and about to
                      press a key for it. Holding the floor costs 40px of a screen that already
                      budgeted them and moves nothing, ever.

                      RESERVED IS NOT DRAWN: with nothing listening the band paints nothing at all —
                      no text, no button, no ground of its own — because a permanent "Press a key"
                      would be an instruction that is false most of the time.

                      THE 32 IS THE BANNER'S OWN HEIGHT, not a designed dimension: `Cancel` is the
                      tallest thing in the row and stands at the control height its theme gives it,
                      so there is no geometry token to name here (the handoff draws no listening
                      bar). Nothing keeps the two numbers in step but the test that keeps this
                      honest — EditorChromeTests.ArmingAKeyForRemap_MovesTheBoardNotAtAll compares
                      the board's window-space bounds armed against unarmed, so a banner that
                      outgrows this floor fails there rather than on somebody's screen.
                    -->
                    <Panel Grid.Row="0"
                           MinHeight="32"
                           Margin="0,0,0,8">
                      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                  Spacing="12"
                                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  IsVisible="{Binding IsListening}">
                        <!-- The banner pulses with the cap it belongs to; the class is bound rather
                             than fixed so the animation only runs while something is actually
                             listening. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Press a key to assign it to the highlighted key."
                                   Classes="control statusWarning"
                                   Classes.recording="{Binding IsListening}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        <Button Content="Cancel"
                                Command="{Binding CancelRemapCommand}"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      </StackPanel>
                    </Panel>

                    <controls:KeyboardView Grid.Row="1"
                                           DataContext="{Binding SelectedLayer}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                           KeySelectedCommand="{Binding $parent[views:KeyboardEditorView].((vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel)DataContext).SelectKeyCommand}" />

                    <!-- ~14px below the board, as the mock spaces it. The row counts the state of
                         the layer on screen and carries `Copy key…` and `Reset layer`. -->
                    <views:BoardLegendView Grid.Row="2"
                                           DataContext="{Binding BoardLegend}"
                                           Margin="0,14,0,0" />
                  </Grid>
                </Panel>

                <!-- The Macros tab (mockup 1i) — a library, not an editor, and since issue #93 it
                     takes the whole content area rather than docking beside the board. It draws no
                     board of its own: `Pick another key` and `＋ Record a macro` both leave for the
                     Layout tab, where the board and the one recording surface in this app are.

                     A bare content host, exactly as the key inspector hosts its own panels, because
                     ViewLocator resolves MacroLibraryViewModel to Views/MacroLibraryView by name. -->
                <ContentControl Content="{Binding MacroLibraryPanel}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding IsMacroLibraryVisible}" />

                <!-- The lighting editor of specs/07-lighting.md §3/§4. Since issue #128 this is the
                     lighting BOARD COLUMN alone — the mode rail beside it is the editor's own rail
                     column, and the lighting layer switch is in the tab-bar row. -->
                <ContentControl Content="{Binding Lighting}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Lighting}" />

                <!-- The keyboard settings of specs/08-settings.md §5; the rows are the device's, so
                     this is one ContentControl and the panel decides what it shows. -->
                <ContentControl Content="{Binding Settings}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Settings}" />
              </Panel>
            </Panel>

            <!-- STILL PROVISIONAL, and it sits under the BOARD now rather than under the board and
                 the rail together (issue #128): it is the section's action row, and the rail is not
                 part of the section.

                 Two more buttons have reached their real homes with the key inspector rail (issue
                 #92): `Reset Key` is now the rail footer's `Revert key`, and `Tap and Hold` is a
                 rail panel rather than a modal — both run the very same commands from there.
                 `Reset Layer` left earlier, for the legend row under the board (#91).

                 `Insert Delay` left with issue #93: §11.3 is edited in place on the key inspector's
                 Macro panel now, so there is no command left for a button here to run.

                 What is left waits for the rest of #93, which owns the editor-level action bar:
                 `Reset Layout`, §11.6's macro-insertion picker, and Import/Export.

                 Nothing here is the design's shape — it exists so every action stays reachable and
                 keeps working in the meantime, on every tab.

                 Every button reports its own availability through its command's CanExecute, so
                 there is no visibility logic here. -->
            <WrapPanel Grid.Row="1" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,12,0,0">
              <Button Content="Reset Layout" Command="{Binding ResetLayoutCommand}" Margin="6,0,0,4" />
              <Button Content="Special Action" Command="{Binding InsertSpecialActionCommand}" Margin="6,0,0,4" />
              <Button Content="Export" Command="{Binding ExportCommand}" Margin="6,0,0,4" />
              <Button Content="Import" Command="{Binding ImportCommand}" Margin="6,0,0,4" />
            </WrapPanel>

            <!-- Lines of the loaded file the parser could not apply (specs/04-layout-files.md
                 §5.2). They are collapsed by default but never dropped silently. -->
            <Expander Grid.Row="2"
                      Margin="0,12,0,0"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      IsVisible="{Binding HasInvalidLines}">
              <Expander.Header>
                <TextBlock Text="Some lines of this profile could not be applied"
                           Classes="control statusWarning" />
              </Expander.Header>
              <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding InvalidLineMessages}" MaxHeight="160">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Spacing="2" />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate DataType="x:String">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" />
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>
            </Expander>
          </Grid>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ===== Column 1: the seam ===========================================================
             6px of hit target over a 1px hairline, all of it the `RailSplitter` control theme's;
             this file names a class and nothing else. It sits in its own Auto column so the
             section's `*` and the rail's fixed width are the only two things sharing the row, which
             is what makes the splitter's "the right column is fixed, so resize only the right
             column" behaviour the one that fires.

             It goes with the rail on the two tabs that have none: an Auto column whose only child
             is invisible measures zero, so the section really does get the whole width. -->
        <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1"
                      Classes="railSplitter"
                      IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter='Keys,Lighting'}"
                      DragDelta="OnRailSplitterDragged"
                      DragCompleted="OnRailSplitterReleased" />

        <!-- ===== Column 2: the rail ===========================================================
             Two faces of one column: the KEY INSPECTOR on the Keys tab (mockups 1e/2a) and the
             LIGHTING MODE RAIL on the Lighting tab (mockup 2f). Neither is modal — no scrim, no
             overlay host, and the board to their left stays clickable the whole time.

             NO GUTTER. The rail carries its own inset surface and a hairline down its left edge
             (`inspectorRail`), which is how mockup 1e separates it — and every pixel spent on a
             margin here comes out of the section's column.

             NO IsVisible ON THE KEY INSPECTOR ITSELF for a selection (issue #119): the rail is
             permanent on the tab it belongs to, and with nothing selected it draws its empty state
             rather than vanishing, so this column never measures zero and the board never shifts
             under the pointer. What IS gated is which of the two rails the column is showing, and
             whether the column exists at all — Macros and Settings have no rail, and the code-behind
             collapses the column to zero for them by watching this host's IsVisible. -->
        <Panel x:Name="RailHost"
               Grid.Column="2"
               IsEnabled="{Binding !IsBusy}"
               IsVisible="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter='Keys,Lighting'}">
          <views:KeyInspectorView
              DataContext="{Binding Inspector}"
              IsVisible="{Binding $parent[views:KeyboardEditorView].((vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel)DataContext).SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Keys}" />

          <views:LightingModeRailView
              DataContext="{Binding Lighting}"
              IsVisible="{Binding $parent[views:KeyboardEditorView].((vm:KeyboardEditorViewModel)DataContext).SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Lighting}" />
        </Panel>
      </Grid>
    </Grid>

    <!-- The feature panels of spec 11, hosted inline rather than as separate windows so keystroke
         capture never has to cross a window boundary. ViewLocator resolves the open overlay's own
         view; the scrim below it swallows every click aimed at the editor underneath. -->
    <Border Classes="overlayScrim"
            Classes.open="{Binding HasActiveOverlay}"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasActiveOverlay}">
      <ContentControl Classes="overlayHost"
                      Classes.open="{Binding HasActiveOverlay}"
                      Content="{Binding ActiveOverlay}"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      Margin="20" />
    </Border>
  </Panel>
</UserControl>
